==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# To [[wait]] until the specified event occurs.
#: {{syn|en|await|wait on|q2=colloquial|Thesaurus:wait for}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Wait for''' the light to change before you cross the street.}}
#: {{ux|en|What are you '''waiting for'''?}}
# To [[await]] the arrival of.
#: {{syn|en|await|wait on|q2=colloquial|Thesaurus:wait for}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''waiting for''' my date.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm '''waiting for''' the train.}}

====Translations====
{{trans-see|await}}

==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|China||generally|_|dated}}
* {{alt|en|chiney|cheny|cheney|chenea|chainy|chaney|chany|chaynee|chayney|cheenie|cheeny|chainé||dialectal}} 

===Etymology===
From clippings of attributive use of {{m|en|China}}, q.v., the country in East Asia. In reference to porcelain and porcelain objects, via clipping of {{m|en|chinaware}} and via this sense of {{der|en|fa|-}} {{fa-l|چِینِی}} in Persia and India, which influenced the pronunciation (see below). In reference to medicine, via clipping of {{m|en|China root}}. In reference to flowers, via clipping of {{m|en|China rose}}. In reference to tea, via clipping of {{m|en|China tea}}. In Cockney slang, a clipping of {{m|en|china plate}} as a rhyme of {{m|en|mate||[[friend]]}}. In reference to drum cymbals, a clipping of {{m|en|China}} {{m|en|cymbal}} and as a genericization of a kind of Zildjian-brand cymbal.<ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=China|part of speech=n.¹ ''and'' adj|code=31735|date=1889}}</ref><ref>{{R:Merriam-Webster|china}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃaɪ̯nə/|[ˈt͡ʃʰaɪ̯nə]}}
** {{audio|en|En-gb-china.oga|a=UK}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-china.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃɑɪ̯nə/|[ˈt͡ʃʰɑ̈ɪ̯nə]|a=AU}}
** {{audio|en|EN-AU ck1 china.ogg}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈt͡ʃeːni/<ref:{{R:en:GDEL|china|734|column=1}} !!! {{R:CPD|China|139|passage=tſa¹'ne¹. ſ.}}>|/ˈt͡ʃiːni/|a=obsolete}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪnə|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|chi|na}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} {{syn of|en|porcelain}}, a [[hard]] [[white]] [[translucent]] [[ceramic]] [[made]] from [[kaolin]], [[now]] {{lb|en|chiefly|_|US}} [[sometimes]] [[distinguished]] in [[reference]] to [[tableware]] as {{m|en|fine}} or {{m|en|good}} ''china''.
#: {{ux|en|It's a [[china doll|'''china''' doll]].}}
# {{senseid|en|chinaware}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[chinaware|Chinaware]]: [[porcelain]] [[tableware]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1634|author=Thomas Herbert|title=A Relation of Some Yeares Trauaile, Begunne Anno 1626. into Afrique and the Greater Asia|page=41
|passage=They sell [[calico|Callicoes]], [[Chinese|Cheney]] [[satin|Sattin]], [[china-ware|'''Cheney''' ware]].}}
#: {{ux|en|He [[set the table]] with '''china''', cloth napkins, and crystal [[stemware]].}}
#: {{ux|en|The traditional 20th anniversary gift is '''china'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|chiefly|_|US|dated}} [[cheaper|Cheaper]] and [[lower]]-[[quality]] [[ceramic]] and [[ceramic]] [[tableware]], [[distinguish]]ed from [[porcelain]].
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=May 11 1921|title=Edison Questions Stir Up a Storm
|passage=What is porcelain? A fine earthenware differing from '''china''' in being harder, whiter, harder to fuse and more translucent than ordinary pottery.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} {{syn of|en|China root}}, the [[root]] of {{taxlink|Smilax china|species}} {{lb|en|particularly}} as a [[medicine]].
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|part=II|section=5|member=1|subsection=v|passage='''China''', saith Manardus, makes a good colour in the face, takes away melancholy, and all infirmities proceeding from cold&nbsp;[…].}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|obsolete}} {{syn of|en|cheyney}}: [[worsted]] or [[woolen]] [[stuff]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1790|author=Alexander Wilson|title=Poems|page=55
|passage=...And then the last boon I'll implore,<br>Is to bless us with China so tight...}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} {{syn of|en|China rose}}, in its [[various]] [[sense]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1844|author=Jane Loudon|title=The Ladies' Companion to the Flower Garden|edition=3rd|page=344
|passage=''Rosa indica'' (the common '''China'''); ''Rosa semperflorens'' (the monthly '''China''').}}
# {{senseid|en|mate}} {{lb|en|countable|Cockney rhyming slang|Australia|South Africa}} {{syn of|en|mate}} {{q|rhyme of china-plate}}.
#: {{ux|en|All right, me old '''china'''?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1880|author=Daniel William Barrett|title=Life and Work among the Navvies|edition=2nd|page=41
|passage=‘Now, then, my [[china-plate|'''china'''-plate]]...’ This is essentially a brick~layer's phrase. If for ‘[[china-plate|'''china'''-plate]]’ you substitute ‘mate’,{{...}} the puzzle is revealed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1925|author=Edward Fraser; et al|title=Soldier and Sailor Words and Phrases|page=53
|passage='''''China''''', or ''Old '''China''':'' chum.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|dated}} [[tea|Tea]] from [[China]], {{lb|en|particularly}} [[varieties]] [[cured]] by [[smoking]] or [[oppose]]d to [[India]]n [[cultivar]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1907|title=Yesterday's Shopping|page=1
|passage=Tea... Finest '''China''', Plain ([[Moning]]).}}
# {{lb|en|countable|games|chiefly|_|US|obsolete}} A [[glazed]] china [[marble]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1932|month=March|author=Dan Beard|title=New-Fashioned Kites and Old-Fashioned Marbles|journal=w:Boys' Life|pageurl=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=Touc6KZnPlYC&pg=PA27|page=27
|passage=The marbles, in those days, had their primitive names. The unglazed china ones were called plasters because they looked like plaster; the glazed china marbles were called '''chinas'''. I remember how charming were the partly colored lines which encircled them.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|music}} A [[kind]] of [[drum]] [[cymbal]] [[approximating]] a [[Chinese]] [[style]] of [[cymbal]], but [[usually]] with [[Turkish]] [[influence]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Carmine Appice|title=Drums for Everyone|pageurl=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=wQeiCwAAQBAJ&pg=PA78|page=78
|passage=China cymbals are a type of short sound cymbal. [Brand X] makes '''chinas''' with really short sounds.}}

